  "image_name": "2a2a02f138344dfc8a2c8a08612cf8fd.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how mesenchymal stem cells support cancer growth at both the primary tumor and metastatic sites. In the primary tumor, stromal cells embedded in the extracellular matrix interact closely with tumor cells and shape the microenvironment through secreted factors such as TGFβ, CCL5, CXCL10, CXCL12, OPN, and PGF. Exosomes are shown as another major communication route. These signals appear to recruit or influence immune-related cells including MDSCs, regulatory T cells, and macrophages, helping create a tumor-promoting niche.\n\nAs the tumor expands, hypoxia develops within the mass, and matrix-remodeling enzymes labeled MMPs are released, suggesting breakdown of surrounding tissue and easier invasion into nearby blood vessels. Tumor cells then enter the circulation and spread to distant organs. Exosomes from the primary tumor also seem to travel ahead to metastatic sites, where they help condition a permissive environment. The right side shows this premetastatic niche involving VEGFR1-positive cells and bone marrow–derived mesenchymal stem cells. At the metastatic site, tumor-associated MSCs surround and support the arriving tumor cells, contributing to establishment of secondary lesions and eventual tumor regrowth.",
